GPU: no glCopyImageSubData on Mesa's v3d (Raspberry Pi): blit instead

Mesa's v3d implements glCopyImageSubData through util_resource_copy_region, on the CPU: both
textures are mapped, untiled, copied and retiled (v3d_resource_transfer_map,
v3d_move_pixels_general_percpp). Workbench's anti-aliasing copies the depth buffer with
GPU_texture_copy() every viewport frame; on a Raspberry Pi 4 (V3D 4.2) at 1322x762 that was about 60%
of all CPU samples while orbiting the default cube (Haiku's profile -f). GLTexture::copy_to() already
has a framebuffer-blit fallback for drivers without ARB_copy_image; use it on V3D: the blit runs on
the GPU.

diff --git a/source/blender/gpu/opengl/gl_backend.cc b/source/blender/gpu/opengl/gl_backend.cc
index 567254b..860c736 100644
--- a/source/blender/gpu/opengl/gl_backend.cc
+++ b/source/blender/gpu/opengl/gl_backend.cc
@@ -351,6 +351,14 @@ static void detect_workarounds()
   {
     GLContext::copy_image_support = false;
   }
+  /* Mesa's v3d (Raspberry Pi) does glCopyImageSubData on the CPU: it maps both textures, untiles,
+   * copies and retiles them. Workbench's anti-aliasing copies the depth buffer every frame that way,
+   * which took about 60% of a viewport frame. The framebuffer blit fallback runs on the GPU. */
+  if (GPU_type_matches(GPU_DEVICE_UNKNOWN, GPU_OS_ANY, GPU_DRIVER_OPENSOURCE) &&
+      strstr(renderer, "V3D"))
+  {
+    GLContext::copy_image_support = false;
+  }
   /* Special fix for these specific GPUs.
    * Without this workaround, blender crashes on startup. (see #72098) */
   if (GPU_type_matches(GPU_DEVICE_INTEL, GPU_OS_WIN, GPU_DRIVER_OFFICIAL) &&
